Hi @burakozdemir32 👋

TL;DR: Treat redirects in tiers.

Exact 1:1 → use redirects() in next.config.js (compiled to a manifest and matched before your app code) or handle at the CDN/reverse-proxy.

Param / wildcard / regex → use Middleware with a precompiled matcher (no per-request regex creation), indexed by prefix so you don’t iterate 1k rules every request. Avoid remote lookups on the hot path.

**1) Exact 1:1 (fastest path)**

next.config.js
`// Thousands of simple redirects are fine; Next compiles these to a fast manifest.
export async function redirects() {
  return [
    { source: "/old-path", destination: "/new-path", permanent: true },
    // ... generate from a CSV/JSON
  ];
}
`
These run before pages/middleware and add negligible TTFB. If you’re on a CDN/reverse proxy (Vercel/Cloudflare/Nginx), you can also put exact rules there.

**2) Param, wildcard, and regex (use Middleware with an indexed matcher)

Idea: Build a prefix index → only check a small subset of rules. Precompile patterns once at module scope.**
`// middleware.ts (Edge Runtime)
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { match } from "path-to-regexp";

// Example rules (could be imported as JSON at build time)
const rules = [
  { src: "/old-products/:id", dst: "/new-shop/:id", code: 308 },
  { src: "/docs/v1/:path*", dst: "/docs/v2/:path*", code: 308 },
  { src: "/blog/:year(\\d{4})/:slug", dst: "/articles/:year/:slug", code: 308 },
];

type Compiled = {
  prefix: string;                         // leading static segment for indexing
  exec: ReturnType<typeof match>;
  dst: string;
  code: number;
};

// Precompile once
const compiled: Record<string, Compiled[]> = {};
for (const r of rules) {
  const m = match(r.src, { decode: decodeURIComponent, strict: false, end: true });
  const prefix = r.src.split("/")[1] ?? ""; // first path segment
  (compiled[prefix] ||= []).push({ prefix, exec: m, dst: r.dst, code: r.code });
}

export function middleware(req: Request) {
  const url = new URL(req.url);
  const seg1 = url.pathname.split("/")[1] ?? "";
  const candidates = (compiled[seg1] || []).concat(compiled[""] || []);

  for (const c of candidates) {
    const res = c.exec(url.pathname);
    if (res) {
      // fill params into dst
      let location = c.dst;
      for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(res.params)) {
        location = location.replace(`:${k}`, Array.isArray(v) ? v.join("/") : String(v));
      }
      return NextResponse.redirect(new URL(location, url), c.code);
    }
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
}

export const config = {
  // only run middleware where needed
  matcher: ["/((?!_next|static|favicon.ico).*)"],
};
`
Why this is fast

- No per-request regex creation (precompiled at module scope).

- Prefix index slashes candidates from 1000 → handful.

- Edge runtime keeps it close to users; no I/O on hot path.

**3) Data-structure tips**

Prefer path-to-regexp (precompiled) over ad-hoc RegExp strings.

Index by first static segment (or longest static prefix) → O(1) bucket lookup.

If patterns share long prefixes (e.g., /docs/...), build a radix trie; libraries like find-my-way (pure JS) do this well.

**4) What not to do**

Don’t evaluate a thousand regexes every request.

Don’t fetch rules from a remote KV/DB on each hit—hydrate at build time or cache in module scope.

Bloom filters help exact matches, but not wildcards/regex—use them only for tier-1 lookups (or skip; the manifest/CDN is already O(1)).

**5) When to use Middleware vs next.config.js**

Use redirects() for exact routes (fastest, zero code).

Use Middleware only for parameterized/wildcard/regex or when redirect logic depends on request context (headers, A/B, geo).

Bottom line:

Keep exact redirects in redirects()/CDN.

Handle complex patterns in Middleware with a precompiled, prefix-indexed matcher.

Avoid per-request iteration and any network I/O.
That combination scales well past 1k+ redirects with minimal TTFB impact.

