## Update: Found an Elegant Solution Using Custom Portal Container

[@bolechen](https://github.com/bolechen) We encountered the same issue with shadcn/ui Sidebar and found a **much better solution** than manual DOM cleanup!

### The Root Cause

When Activity sets the route to "hidden", it applies `display: none` to the layout wrapper. However, **Portals mounted to `document.body` are NOT affected** by this, causing them to remain visible.

```javascript
// Test result: Portal not managed by Activity
{
  "sidebar-wrapper": {
    "display": "none !important"  // ✅ Layout elements correctly hidden
  },
  "sidebar-portal": {
    "display": "flex",             // ❌ Portal still visible!
    "isVisible": true
  }
}
```

### Our Solution: Custom Portal Container

Instead of manual DOM cleanup, we **move the Portal into the layout** so Activity can manage it automatically.

**Step 1: Make Sidebar accept a custom Portal container** (minimal changes to shadcn/ui components)

```typescript
// src/components/ui/sidebar.tsx
import * as SheetPrimitive from "@radix-ui/react-dialog"
import { Sheet, SheetPortal, SheetOverlay } from "@/components/ui/sheet"

function Sidebar({ 
  portalContainer,  // ✅ New optional prop
  ...props 
}: React.ComponentProps<"div"> & {
  portalContainer?: HTMLElement | null
}) {
  if (isMobile) {
    return (
      <Sheet open={openMobile} onOpenChange={setOpenMobile}>
        {/* 🎯 Use Radix UI's native container prop */}
        <SheetPortal container={portalContainer}>
          <SheetOverlay />
          <SheetPrimitive.Content {...contentProps}>
            {children}
          </SheetPrimitive.Content>
        </SheetPortal>
      </Sheet>
    )
  }
}
```

**Step 2: Create a container ref in the route group layout**

```typescript
// src/app/(read)/_components/layout/read-sidebar.tsx
export const ReadSidebar = ({ ... }) => {
  const portalContainerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  return (
    <div ref={portalContainerRef}>  {/* Portal mount point */}
      <Sidebar portalContainer={portalContainerRef.current} {...props}>
        {/* Sidebar content */}
      </Sidebar>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Why This Works

**Before:**
```html
<body>
  <div id="__next">
    <div style="display: none !important">  <!-- Activity hidden -->
      <div ref={containerRef}></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div data-radix-portal>  <!-- ❌ Still visible! -->
    <dialog>Sidebar content</dialog>
  </div>
</body>
```

**After:**
```html
<body>
  <div id="__next">
    <div style="display: none !important">  <!-- Activity hidden -->
      <div ref={containerRef}>
        <div data-radix-portal>  <!-- ✅ Hidden with container! -->
          <dialog>Sidebar content</dialog>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
```

### Benefits

✅ **No manual DOM manipulation** - relies entirely on React and Next.js built-in mechanisms  
✅ **Respects React architecture** - Portal still managed by React component tree  
✅ **Automatic cleanup** - Activity hidden mechanism sets `display: none` automatically  
✅ **No race conditions** - no need to handle React cleanup timing issues  
✅ **No delays** - works immediately, no 500ms timeout needed  
✅ **Minimal changes** - only adds an optional prop, fully backward compatible  

### Testing

- ✅ Open mobile Sidebar in `/r/[pageId]`
- ✅ Navigate to `/all` (cross-layout)
- ✅ Dialog elements completely cleaned up
- ✅ Visual check: page is clean, no leftover UI

This approach leverages **Radix UI's native `container` prop** (officially supported) and **Next.js 16's Activity component** working together as designed.

Hope this helps! 🎉

