The inconsistent data you are seeing after `revalidateTag` is caused by **two separate caching layers** that are easy to conflate:

### 1. Server-side fetch/route cache
This is what `revalidateTag` invalidates. When your revalidation endpoint fires, the Next.js server discards the cached fetch result and will re-fetch from your data source on the next incoming request. This works correctly in your setup.

### 2. Client-side Router Cache (in-memory)
This is the culprit. Next.js keeps an in-memory cache of RSC payloads in the browser for pages the user has already visited. Even after the server has fresh data, the browser may serve the previously cached page RSC payload on navigation — without hitting the server at all. This explains:
- Seeing stale data on some pages but not others (depends on which pages are cached in the router)
- Needing ~15 minutes for everything to sync (that is the default `staleTimes` expiry for static pages: 300 seconds)
- Needing to refresh twice (first load hits router cache, explicit reload forces a full network request)

### Fix

Configure `staleTimes` in `next.config.ts` to reduce how long the client-side router cache holds on to static page payloads:

```ts
// next.config.ts
import type { NextConfig } from "next";

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    staleTimes: {
      static: 30,  // seconds — was 300 (5 min) by default
      dynamic: 0,  // seconds — keep at 0 for dynamic routes
    },
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
```

Alternatively, if a page shows content that changes after mutations (like your post list and post detail), call `router.refresh()` right after the mutation/revalidation fires on the client:

```ts
import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";

const router = useRouter();

async function handlePublish() {
  await fetch("/api/revalidate", { method: "POST" });
  router.refresh(); // forces the router cache to be bypassed for the current page
}
```

`router.refresh()` does not do a full page reload — it re-requests the current route from the server and updates the router cache in place, so the user stays on the same page but sees fresh content immediately.

### Summary

| Layer | Invalidated by | Your issue? |
|---|---|---|
| Fetch / ISR cache | `revalidateTag` / `revalidatePath` | No — working correctly |
| Client Router Cache | `router.refresh()` or `staleTimes` expiry | **Yes** — this is the cause |

Reducing `staleTimes.static` to something like `30`–`60` seconds, or calling `router.refresh()` after publishing/editing, should resolve the inconsistency immediately.

