Good catch — this is a real problem. Here are a few approaches, from simplest to most flexible:

## 1. Move JSON-LD outside Suspense boundaries (simplest)

The key insight: JSON-LD doesn't need to live inside the component that fetches the data. You can fetch the data at the page level (outside `<Suspense>`) and render JSON-LD there, while still streaming the visual UI:

```tsx
// app/product/[id]/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import { getProduct } from '@/lib/data'

export default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  // This fetch runs before streaming starts — blocks the shell
  const product = await getProduct(params.id)

  const jsonLd = {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Product',
    name: product.name,
    description: product.description,
    // ...
  }

  return (
    <>
      {/* JSON-LD is in the initial HTML shell — visible to all crawlers */}
      <script
        type="application/ld+json"
        dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: JSON.stringify(jsonLd) }}
      />

      {/* Heavy UI still streams */}
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading reviews...</div>}>
        <ProductReviews productId={params.id} />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  )
}
```

**Tradeoff:** The initial shell waits for the data fetch, but JSON-LD is always in the first HTML response. If the fetch is fast (cached / `'use cache'`), this is negligible.

## 2. Use middleware to disable streaming for bot user agents

If you want to keep the current architecture but serve a fully-rendered page to AI crawlers:

```typescript
// middleware.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server'

const AI_BOT_PATTERN = /GPTBot|CCBot|anthropic-ai|ChatGPT-User|Claude-Web|Bytespider|PetalBot/i

export function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  const ua = request.headers.get('user-agent') || ''

  if (AI_BOT_PATTERN.test(ua)) {
    // Set a header that your layout/page can read
    const response = NextResponse.next()
    response.headers.set('x-is-bot', '1')
    return response
  }
}
```

Then in your layout, read the header and conditionally avoid Suspense:

```tsx
import { headers } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Layout({ children }) {
  const headersList = await headers()
  const isBot = headersList.get('x-is-bot') === '1'

  // Pass isBot to a context or use it to conditionally render
  // When isBot is true, don't wrap components in Suspense
}
```

## 3. Use `generateMetadata` for JSON-LD in `<head>`

While non-standard placement (JSON-LD is typically in `<body>`), Google and most parsers accept JSON-LD in `<head>`. Since `generateMetadata` resolves before streaming:

```tsx
export async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const product = await getProduct(params.id)
  
  return {
    other: {
      'script:ld+json': JSON.stringify({
        '@context': 'https://schema.org',
        '@type': 'Product',
        name: product.name,
      }),
    },
  }
}
```

> **Note:** This approach depends on how Next.js renders the `other` metadata field — you may need a custom `<head>` injection approach depending on your version.

---

**My recommendation:** Go with **approach #1** for most cases. It's the simplest, doesn't require middleware tricks, and the data fetch for JSON-LD is usually fast (especially with caching). Reserve approach #2 for cases where you absolutely need full streaming for human visitors but still want AI crawlers to see everything.

