To add to the excellent explanation above — the core issue is that Next.js 14 with `generateMetadata()` uses **React's streaming architecture**, which means the `<title>` tag is injected via a deferred RSC payload rather than being present in the initial HTML flush. Here's a practical breakdown and the most reliable fixes:

## Why this happens with ISR specifically

With `dynamic = "force-static"` + `revalidate = 86400`, Next.js serves a **stale cached page** while regenerating in the background. During the brief regeneration window:

1. The stale page is served from cache — title renders fine
2. A new page is being built — if Googlebot hits during this window, it may get the streaming HTML where `<title>` hasn't been inlined yet
3. This is why it's **random** — it only happens when Googlebot crawls during a revalidation cycle

## Most reliable fix for your setup

Since you're on Next.js 14.2.x with ISR, the cleanest solution without upgrading is to **move metadata out of streaming** by converting `generateMetadata` to use only synchronous/cached data:

```typescript
// Instead of fetching inside generateMetadata (which defers it),
// ensure the data is already cached before metadata resolves
export async function generateMetadata({ params }: Props): Promise<Metadata> {
  // Use unstable_cache to ensure this resolves before streaming starts
  const getData = unstable_cache(
    async () => fetchPageData(params.slug),
    [params.slug],
    { revalidate: 86400 }
  );
  
  const data = await getData();
  
  return {
    title: data.title,
    description: data.description,
  };
}
```

If the metadata fetch itself is slow (causes streaming to defer), this is the likely culprit.

## Quick diagnostic

Run this to see exactly what Googlebot sees:

```bash
curl -s -A "Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Googlebot/2.1; +http://www.google.com/bot.html)" \
  https://your-site.com/zh-tw/suggestion/city/taitung-car-rental \
  | grep -i '<title>'
```

If you see `<title></title>` (empty), your metadata is definitely deferred.

## Summary of fixes by effort level

| Fix | Effort | SEO Impact |
|---|---|---|
| `generateStaticParams` for known routes | Low | Best — title in static HTML |
| `unstable_cache` wrap on metadata fetch | Low | Prevents streaming deferral |
| Fallback `<title>` in root `layout.tsx` | Very low | Prevents *empty* title |
| Upgrade to Next.js 15+ | Medium | Fixes root cause (PR #64961) |
| Move to SSR (`dynamic = "force-dynamic"`) | Medium | No stale cache window |

The **fastest no-upgrade fix** that directly addresses your ISR + revalidation scenario: add a fallback `<title>` in `layout.tsx` AND wrap your metadata fetch in `unstable_cache`. This ensures Googlebot always sees a non-empty title even if it hits during a revalidation window.

