This is a known behavior with Next.js's **streaming metadata** feature. When `generateMetadata` performs an async `fetch()`, the metadata resolution can complete *after* the initial HTML shell has been streamed to the client. When that happens, Next.js injects the `<title>` and `<meta>` tags into the `<body>` via a client-side script that moves them to `<head>` during hydration.

**If you view-source** (not DevTools), you'll likely see the tags in the body. After JavaScript hydration, they get moved to `<head>` — so search engines that execute JS (Googlebot) will see them correctly. However, bots that only parse raw HTML (like Facebook's crawler) may not.

This has been reported in several issues:
- [#79313](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/79313) — Metadata renders inside body after v15.3.1
- [#83871](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/83871) — generateMetadata renders meta in body

**Workarounds:**

1. **Add `loading.tsx`** to the route — this creates a Suspense boundary that lets Next.js wait for metadata before streaming:
```tsx
// app/shared/[shareId]/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  return null; // or a skeleton UI
}
```

2. **Cache the fetch** so metadata resolves faster (and potentially before the shell is sent):
```tsx
const post = await fetch(`${API_URL}/api/${shareId}`, {
  next: { revalidate: 60 }
}).then(res => res.json());
```

3. **Use `connection()` from `next/server`** to force dynamic rendering and ensure metadata resolves before the response starts streaming:
```tsx
import { connection } from 'next/server';

export async function generateMetadata({ params }: Props): Promise<Metadata> {
  await connection();
  const shareId = (await params).shareId;
  const post = await fetch(`${API_URL}/api/${shareId}`).then(res => res.json());
  return {
    title: `${APP_NAME} - ${post.title}`,
    description: '',
  };
}
```

Also double-check your root `layout.tsx` has the proper `<html>` and `<body>` tags — missing these can cause metadata injection to break.

